      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="margin-top: 50px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">Why are water-based aerosols more prone to corrosion than solvent-based ones?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Corrosion of steel is an electrochemical process that requires an electrolyte, and water is an excellent one. A solvent-based formulation provides very little ionic conductivity, so the anodic and cathodic reactions that drive metal loss cannot proceed readily. Introducing water, especially with dissolved salts or ionic surfactants, supplies exactly the conductive medium those reactions need.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What role does the internal lacquer play?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The internal lacquer is a barrier coating that physically separates the aqueous formulation from the metal substrate. Corrosion typically begins wherever that barrier is imperfect, such as at coating pinholes, scratches from handling, or the cut edge of a seam. Because the exposed area at a defect is very small relative to the surrounding coated metal, attack concentrates there and can perforate the wall locally.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">How does pH affect corrosion in an aerosol can?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Both strongly acidic and strongly alkaline conditions increase attack on the metals used in aerosol containers. Aluminium is amphoteric, meaning its protective oxide film dissolves at both low and high pH, so it is particularly sensitive to formulations that drift outside a near-neutral range. Controlling and buffering formulation pH is therefore one of the most direct levers available for corrosion control.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What is crevice corrosion and where does it occur in an aerosol?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Crevice corrosion occurs in narrow gaps where the liquid becomes stagnant and its chemistry diverges from the bulk formulation, typically becoming more acidic and oxygen-depleted. In an aerosol container the classic locations are the crimp region under the valve cup, the side seam, and beneath the gasket. These areas are difficult to inspect, so failures often appear only after extended storage.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">How is corrosion resistance tested before launch?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The standard approach is accelerated storage: filled cans are held at elevated temperature for a defined period and then opened and examined for metal attack, coating breakdown, and changes in the product. Salt spray exposure to methods such as ASTM B117 is used to compare coating systems on panels rather than filled units. Neither test predicts shelf life exactly, so results are used comparatively against a known-good control.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">Can corrosion cause an aerosol can to leak or burst?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Yes. Localised attack such as pitting can perforate the can wall, producing a slow leak that may appear months after filling. Corrosion also generates hydrogen at the cathodic sites, which adds to the internal pressure the container must contain. A wall thinned by corrosion combined with elevated pressure reduces the safety margin the can was designed with.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

